{
  "concept": "Electrophilic bromination of benzene proceeds through a high-energy cationic intermediate and two activation barriers, with substitution favored because it restores aromaticity and ends downhill while addition would end uphill.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "pale blue rectangular background panel containing the full reaction coordinate diagram",
    "vertical energy arrow at far left with yellow-to-orange gradient fill and black label 'Energy'",
    "horizontal reaction progress label beneath the panel",
    "magenta right-pointing reaction progress arrow beneath the panel",
    "dashed horizontal reactant energy level on the left",
    "reactant benzene ring structure at lower left",
    "plus sign between reactants",
    "magenta reactant label 'Br2'",
    "black substitution reaction energy curve beginning at the reactant level, rising to a high first transition-state peak, dropping to the cationic intermediate trough, rising to a lower second transition-state peak, and falling to the lower product level",
    "short dashed horizontal marker across the first transition-state peak",
    "vertical double-headed arrow from reactant energy level to first transition-state peak labeled 'ΔG‡'",
    "cationic intermediate structure at the trough: six-membered ring with partial unsaturation, H and magenta Br attached to the same carbon, and a plus sign above the ring",
    "black substitution second transition-state peak after the cationic intermediate",
    "dashed horizontal substitution product energy level below the reactant level",
    "small vertical double-headed arrow between reactant level and substitution product level labeled 'ΔG°'",
    "substitution product bromobenzene structure at lower right with magenta Br substituent",
    "plus sign beside substitution product",
    "product label 'HBr' with Br in magenta",
    "label 'Substitution' below the substitution products",
    "magenta addition pathway curve branching from the cationic intermediate trough, rising to its own transition-state maximum, and ending above the reactant level",
    "bracketed addition product structure at upper right: nonaromatic dibrominated cyclohexadiene-like ring with H and Br substituents",
    "label 'Addition'",
    "magenta label '(does not occur)' beneath Addition",
    "highlight marker that can move along the active reaction pathway",
    "glow or emphasis style for the currently active molecular structure",
    "highlight overlays for ΔG‡ and ΔG° energy arrows and their dashed start/end levels"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "reactionStage",
      "type": "state",
      "label": "Reaction stage",
      "states": [
        "reactants",
        "first transition state",
        "cationic intermediate",
        "second transition state",
        "products"
      ],
      "default": "reactants",
      "teaches": "Electrophilic bromination is not a single downhill drop: it passes through a high first activation barrier, a cationic intermediate, and a second barrier before substitution products form"
    },
    {
      "id": "pathwayChoice",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Pathway",
      "options": [
        "Substitution",
        "Addition"
      ],
      "default": "Substitution",
      "teaches": "The shared cationic intermediate can in principle lead to two products, but substitution is favored because its endpoint is lower in energy while addition remains higher in energy and does not occur"
    },
    {
      "id": "energyArrowFocus",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Activation free energy ΔG‡ measures the climb from reactants to a transition state, whereas overall free energy ΔG° measures the net energy change from reactants to products"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Start with benzene and bromine",
      "narration": "The reactants begin on the left dashed energy level: benzene plus Br2. The vertical placement sets the reference energy for comparing both possible outcomes.",
      "state": {
        "reactionStage": "reactants",
        "pathwayChoice": "Substitution"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Climb the first barrier",
      "narration": "The black curve rises to a high first transition state. The ΔG‡ arrow marks the activation free energy required to reach this unstable arrangement.",
      "state": {
        "reactionStage": "first transition state",
        "pathwayChoice": "Substitution"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Form the cationic intermediate",
      "narration": "After the first barrier, the reaction reaches a carbocation intermediate. The ring has temporarily lost aromatic stabilization, so this intermediate remains relatively high in energy.",
      "state": {
        "reactionStage": "cationic intermediate",
        "pathwayChoice": "Substitution"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Cross the second barrier",
      "narration": "The substitution pathway climbs a smaller second barrier as the intermediate moves toward restoring aromaticity. This second peak is lower than the first transition state.",
      "state": {
        "reactionStage": "second transition state",
        "pathwayChoice": "Substitution"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Substitution restores aromaticity",
      "narration": "The black pathway falls to bromobenzene plus HBr, below the reactant energy level. The negative overall ΔG° shows why substitution is the observed pathway.",
      "state": {
        "reactionStage": "products",
        "pathwayChoice": "Substitution"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare the addition branch",
      "narration": "Switching to the magenta branch shows that addition would end at a higher-energy, nonaromatic product. Because that endpoint is uphill, the addition product is labeled as not occurring.",
      "state": {
        "reactionStage": "products",
        "pathwayChoice": "Addition"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Both pathways share the same high-energy intermediate, but substitution wins because it restores aromaticity and ends lower in energy, while addition destroys aromaticity and ends uphill.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Recreate the original as a pale blue reaction-coordinate panel with energy increasing upward and reaction progress left to right. Use a thick black curve for substitution and a thick magenta curve branching from the intermediate trough for addition. Keep the reactant level dashed on the left, the substitution product level dashed lower on the right, and the addition endpoint visibly higher than the reactants. Color all bromine labels magenta. The stage control should move a bright marker along the chosen curve and glow the corresponding molecular drawing. The pathway toggle should keep both pathways faintly visible but make the selected branch and endpoint bold. Clicking ΔG‡ or ΔG° should emphasize the chosen double-headed arrow and its start/end energy levels so the two energy quantities are visually distinct."
}

